    "image": "/animal-images/white-tailed-deer.jpg",
    "summary": "White-tailed deer are medium-sized, slender-bodied deer with a long tail that flashes bright white underneath when alarmed—often the first clear “field mark” a visitor notices. In summer, their coat is more reddish-brown; in winter it shifts to a grayer fawn tone. Adult males (bucks) grow antlers with points branching from a main beam, while females usually have none. Tracks are heart-shaped with two sharp hooves; in soft ground you may also see dewclaw marks behind.",
    "description": "White-tailed deer are medium-sized, slender-bodied deer with a long tail that flashes bright white underneath when alarmed—often the first clear “field mark” a visitor notices. In summer, their coat is more reddish-brown; in winter it shifts to a grayer fawn tone. Adult males (bucks) grow antlers with points branching from a main beam, while females usually have none. Tracks are heart-shaped with two sharp hooves; in soft ground you may also see dewclaw marks behind.\n\nDeer growth and seasonal behavior are closely tied to food and breeding cycles: bucks shed antlers annually, and breeding activity (the rut) peaks in fall to early winter with timing that varies by region. Deer are crepuscular—most active near sunrise and sunset—so visitors often see them moving quietly between cover and feeding areas at those times.",
    "habitat": "In Central Texas, white-tailed deer thrive anywhere brushy cover and woodland edges provide both hiding places and nearby forage. They rest during the day in thickets or protected spots and often feed into the night when conditions are calm. Their home ranges are typically small when food and water are reliable, which is why the same neighborhood greenbelt can host the same individuals for months or years.",
    "parkLocation": "Look for deer along brushy trail edges, woodland openings, and quiet stretches of riparian corridor—especially at sunrise and the hour before dark, when they step into open lawns, meadows, or mowed clearings to graze.",
    "ecologicalRole": "As abundant herbivores, deer shape plant communities through browsing and grazing. Where deer numbers are high, they can reduce regeneration of some native shrubs and trees and shift what plant species dominate understories and wildflower patches. They also move nutrients across the landscape as they travel between bedding cover and feeding areas.\n- Influences which native plants successfully regenerate\n- Provides prey (especially fawns and carrion) for predators and scavengers\n- Moves seeds and nutrients across habitats through feeding and droppings",
    "funFact": "- Antler point-counts are not a reliable way to tell a buck’s age; nutrition and genetics strongly affect antler size.\n- Deer are often easiest to spot in the hour before dark because activity rises just before sunset."
