    "image": "/animal-images/coyote.jpg",
    "summary": "Coyotes are medium-sized, doglike carnivores with a slender build, pointed ears, a narrow muzzle, and a bushy tail often tipped darker. Their coat typically looks gray-buff with black “peppering,” blending into dry grasses, leaf litter, and brush. In the field, you may glimpse just a smooth, low, trotting silhouette crossing a trail corridor. Tracks resemble a small dog’s but tend to be more oval and direct in travel lines, reflecting their efficient movement.",
    "description": "Coyotes are medium-sized, doglike carnivores with a slender build, pointed ears, a narrow muzzle, and a bushy tail often tipped darker. Their coat typically looks gray-buff with black “peppering,” blending into dry grasses, leaf litter, and brush. In the field, you may glimpse just a smooth, low, trotting silhouette crossing a trail corridor. Tracks resemble a small dog’s but tend to be more oval and direct in travel lines, reflecting their efficient movement.\n\nCoyotes are highly adaptable in diet and behavior, which is a big reason they persist in and around cities. Their food changes with season and opportunity—rodents and rabbits are important, but they will also eat fruit and carrion and take advantage of human-altered landscapes. This flexibility helps coyotes occupy everything from big preserves to smaller neighborhood greenbelts.",
    "habitat": "In Central Texas parks, coyotes use a patchwork of habitats: brushy edges, creek corridors, open fields for hunting, and dense cover for daytime shelter. They can cross developed areas using “green threads” like drainage easements and riparian strips. Because they move widely, they may be present even if rarely seen—often detected instead by tracks, scat, or nighttime vocalizations.",
    "parkLocation": "Coyotes most often travel along quiet, linear routes—creekside trails, utility corridors, and the transition zone where woodland meets meadow. Early morning and late evening are prime viewing times, though they may also move during daylight on cool, overcast days.",
    "ecologicalRole": "Coyotes function as top predators in many Central Texas urban-wildland mosaics. By hunting rodents and rabbits and scavenging carcasses, they influence prey populations and help recycle nutrients. Importantly, predator removal can sometimes increase conflict by opening territory to younger dispersing coyotes, which may behave differently than established adults.\n- Helps regulate rodent and rabbit populations\n- Scavenges carrion, accelerating nutrient recycling\n- Shapes wildlife behavior (prey may alter foraging to avoid predation risk)",
    "funFact": "- Coyotes can shift their diet dramatically by season—more carrion in winter, more rodents and fruit in warmer months.\n- They are one of North America’s most successful “urban adapters,” routinely using cities as habitat."
