    "description": "Bobcats are compact wild cats with a short “bobbed” tail—black on top at the tip and paler below—plus tufted ears and a facial ruff that can make the head look broad. Their coat is reddish-brown to grayish with black streaking and spots; the back of each ear is notably dark with a contrasting pale patch. Most sightings are brief: a low, silent animal slipping into cover, often at dusk or at night.\n\nBobcats are active year-round and do not hibernate. They hunt primarily by stalking and pouncing, relying on sharp hearing and vision. In Central Texas, rabbits are a key food source, with other prey—including rodents and squirrels—supplementing as available. Their ability to use agricultural and urban edges (where cover remains) helps them persist near people.",
