    "summary": "Nine-banded armadillos are unmistakable: their back and sides are protected by a bony “armor” (carapace) with large shoulder and rump shields and nine flexible bands between. They have strong digging claws—especially on the front feet—and a long, tapering tail ringed with bony plates. Color is typically brownish with scattered pale hairs, and their peg-like teeth reflect a diet focused on invertebrates rather than chewing tough plant matter.",
    "description": "Nine-banded armadillos are unmistakable: their back and sides are protected by a bony “armor” (carapace) with large shoulder and rump shields and nine flexible bands between. They have strong digging claws—especially on the front feet—and a long, tapering tail ringed with bony plates. Color is typically brownish with scattered pale hairs, and their peg-like teeth reflect a diet focused on invertebrates rather than chewing tough plant matter.\n\nArmadillos are built for digging and foraging by smell, often rooting in soil and leaf litter at night. They are common in central and eastern Texas and are strongly influenced by soil characteristics: areas with more easily dug soils tend to support higher densities. Many park visitors notice them as “moving mounds,” heard rustling in leaves before they’re seen.",
