    "parkLocation": "Largemouth bass are most likely found in quiet coves of park lakes, slow creek pools behind dams or rock bars, and deep holes along shaded banks. In spring, males can be seen guarding shallow sandy nests near pond edges or creek shoals. Trail signs might point out fishing piers or boathouses on park lakes where bass are often caught.",
    "ecologicalRole": "As top warmwater predators, largemouth bass help regulate populations of smaller fish (like sunfish, minnows, and young catfish) and aquatic invertebrates. By controlling these prey populations, they contribute to a balanced ecosystem and help prevent any one species from overpopulating. Bass also serve as an important food source for great blue herons, otters, and humans.  \n- Preys on smaller fish, amphibians, and large aquatic insects, keeping those populations in check.  \n- Creates nesting and feeding opportunities for other species (nesting bass stir up sediment, which some small fish exploit).  \n- Supports recreational fishing—a reason many parks actively manage lake and creek habitats.",
