    "parkLocation": "In parks with streams or lakes, channel catfish lurk in deep pools and under overhanging banks. They are less likely to be seen but often caught by fishermen using bait on or near the bottom. In shallower ponds, look near stumps or rocky edges after dark. Signs might mark “no fishing” zones to protect young catfish while they feed at night in shallow areas.",
    "ecologicalRole": "Channel catfish are important bottom-feeders and scavengers. They consume a variety of food — insects, crayfish, snails, smaller fish, and organic debris — and help clean up the ecosystem by eating dead material. They in turn support wildlife: many birds (e.g. herons, egrets, kingfishers) and otters feed on catfish, and a single adult catfish can contain parasites and nutrients that feed many other creatures when eaten.  \n- Recycles nutrients by scavenging on the bottom, keeping waterways cleaner.  \n- Provides sport and food fish resources for humans (contributing to wildlife-watching and fishing).  \n- Serves as a prey item for wildlife (otters, raccoons, herons) that forage in creeks.",
