    "summary": "Common carp are large, heavy-bodied fish introduced from Europe. They can reach 2 feet or more and have bronze-gold scales that shimmer in the sun. Carp have fleshy lips (often seen foraging in mud) and a pair of small barbels (whisker-like feelers) near the mouth. They are omnivores: if you see one, it’s often with mouth down in the sediment, sucking up plants, insects, and small creatures. They can live a long time (over 20 years) and grow very large in warm, rich waters.",
    "description": "Common carp are large, heavy-bodied fish introduced from Europe. They can reach 2 feet or more and have bronze-gold scales that shimmer in the sun. Carp have fleshy lips (often seen foraging in mud) and a pair of small barbels (whisker-like feelers) near the mouth. They are omnivores: if you see one, it’s often with mouth down in the sediment, sucking up plants, insects, and small creatures. They can live a long time (over 20 years) and grow very large in warm, rich waters.",
    "habitat": "Carp thrive in ponds, lakes, and slow rivers, especially those with muddy bottoms. They prefer warm, nutrient-rich waters and can tolerate low oxygen. Carp often stir up bottom sediment while feeding, which increases water turbidity (cloudiness). In parks, carp are common where warm lakes or impoundments exist. They avoid fast currents, so look for them in backwaters, pond margins, and behind in-park dams.",
    "parkLocation": "Look for common carp in shallow lake edges and slow backwaters. They often surface or come near shore when feeding – you might even hear a “gulping” sound. Parks with weedy ponds or fishing lakes often host carp; signs like “wildlife refuge” might note their presence. Young carp school in shallow weeds, while big carp wallow in deeper mud.",
    "ecologicalRole": "As a non-native species, carp have a mixed impact. They feed on aquatic plants and invertebrates, often reducing plant cover and muddying the water. This can harm native plants and fish that need clear water. However, they also serve as food for large predators (alligator gar, birds). In educational signage, carp are often used to discuss invasive species control and habitat restoration.  \n- Dig up sediments and uproot plants, which can release nutrients and cause algae blooms (a negative effect on water quality).  \n- Serve as large-bodied prey for fish-eating birds (e.g., herons, pelicans) and some big fish.  \n- Can indicate ecosystem imbalance; their presence often leads parks to manage vegetation and water clarity.",
    "funFact": "- Carp can breathe air from the surface, allowing them to survive in very low-oxygen water.  \n- An ancient fish: carp have been farmed for thousands of years in Asia (they’re related to koi).  \n- They can dramatically change a pond: sometimes carp are removed to help restore clear water and native plants."
